1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,980
This is the brief on conceptual database design and ER modeling.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:10,060
Think of us as your personal, seasoned database designers, here to tutor you for that upcoming DBMS exam.

3
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:15,380
Because honestly, mastering ER diagrams is literally the ultimate cheat code for database design.

4
00:00:15,860 --> 00:00:19,120
First, let's nail down that foundational geometric vocabulary.

5
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:22,560
I always like to compare these shapes to parts of speech, right?

6
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:26,180
So your rectangles are the entity sets, basically your main nouns.

7
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:29,600
The diamonds, those are relationship sets, which act as your verbs.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,980
And the ellipses are attributes, serving as your adjectives.

9
00:00:33,380 --> 00:00:36,240
Oh, and keep an eye out for double ellipses for multi-valued attributes,

10
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:38,280
like if someone has multiple phone numbers,

11
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:41,120
and dashed ellipses for derived attributes.

12
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:45,820
Second, how do we actually choose what becomes an entity versus an attribute?

13
00:00:46,440 --> 00:00:51,340
Well, as a rule of thumb, if a thing has its own internal attributes or a dynamic life cycle,

14
00:00:51,540 --> 00:00:52,780
it's definitely an entity.

15
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:56,380
But if it's just a simple atomic fact, it's an attribute.

16
00:00:57,100 --> 00:01:03,800
And hey, why go through the trouble of computing a derived attribute, like figuring out age from a date of birth, instead of just storing the age?

17
00:01:04,380 --> 00:01:08,560
To avoid data staleness, you compute it on the fly so it's absolutely never outdated.

18
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:12,780
Finally, we need to map how everything interacts using cardinalities.

19
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:16,400
One to one, one to many, many to one, and many to many.

20
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:23,080
When you're drawing these, remember that a directed arrow acts like a strict bouncer, enforcing an at-most one rule.

21
00:01:23,680 --> 00:01:30,660
So an arrow pointing to departments in a managers relationship simply means that department has at most one manager.

22
00:01:31,160 --> 00:01:39,460
When you're under the gun during that exam, always draw your clean core entity rectangles and relationship diamonds first before you clutter up the whole page with attribute ovals.
